# Build context is the repo root (docker build -f environment/Dockerfile .).
# Exclude everything not needed for the image to keep the context small + cache-friendly.
**/.git
**/target
**/.slim
**/dev-docs
**/docs
**/tests
**/admin-ui
**/*.rs
**/Cargo.toml
**/Cargo.lock
**/.github
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/*.md
**/.gitignore
**/.ignore
**/node_modules

# The whole build context is uploaded to the builder even though this Dockerfile
# only COPYs `bin/hydra`, so local secrets and per-machine run-time state must not
# travel with it: `secure/` holds TLS material plus a local-test env file, and the
# SQLite/pid files can contain tenant rows. (`**/*.md` above already covers
# LOCAL_TEST_ENV.md / CODE_REVIEW.md; they are repeated because those two are the
# files most likely to be renamed away from `.md`.)
**/secure
**/*.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
LOCAL_TEST_ENV.md
CODE_REVIEW.md
**/*.pid
**/*.db
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-journal
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Local AI-agent state. `.gitignore` already lists these as "never commit", and
# they routinely hold session transcripts (which quote source, config and
# sometimes credentials). They are absent from a clean checkout, so this only
# matters on a developer machine — which is exactly where `docker build` runs.
**/.claude
**/.opencode
**/.agents
**/.cursor
**/.windsurf
**/.continue
**/.codeium
**/.aider*

# Playwright artifacts from a local UI-E2E run (CI uploads them; a local run just
# leaves them in the build context).
**/test-results
**/playwright-report

# Local caches and acceptance-run leftovers. `.acceptance/` + `.cargo-cache/` are
# ~1.4 GB on a machine that has run the gates, and NOTHING excluded them, so every
# `environment/build.sh` uploaded them alongside a single 32 MB binary (measured
# context: ~1.28 GB). None of it is referenced by the Dockerfile.
**/.acceptance
**/.cargo-cache
**/.docker
**/.buildx
**/data
**/environment/bin

# Defensive no-ops, kept deliberately: NOTHING above excludes `bin/`, so
# `bin/hydra` (staged by environment/build.sh, and needed by the Dockerfile's
# `COPY bin/hydra`) is already included — measured: deleting these two lines
# changes nothing. They are here so that a future rule excluding `bin/` cannot
# silently break the image build. The comment used to read "KEEP the staged
# binary", which implied they were load-bearing.
!bin/
!bin/hydra
